WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:04.420
We go to the movies for a lot of reasons,
but most of them can be boiled down to our

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:07.770
desire to feel or experience something that
we wouldn't be able to otherwise.

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:12.280
The best filmmakers can put us directly in
touch with these emotions and sensations,

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:13.380
and it can be pretty great.

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:17.910
It can also be pretty seriously distressing
when skilled filmmakers use their gifts to

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:21.780
punch us straight in the gut, particularly
when that punch is unexpected.

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:25.500
These are films that promised us a rollicking
good time, and they delivered.

00:00:25.509 --> 00:00:30.130
But along with the thrills and chills came
moments of pure, unadulterated trauma that

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:32.160
lingered long after the credits rolled.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:34.320
Star Wars: The Force Awakens

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:39.840
Star Wars: Episode VII The Force Awakens was
generally seen as a satisfying return to form

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:42.600
for the series after the plodding lows of
the prequels.

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:47.480
While the story it told was occasionally criticized
as being not much more than a rehash of the

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:53.440
1977 original, it also gave us memorable new
characters like the malevolent Kylo Ren, Force-sensitive

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:56.519
scavenger Rey, and renegade stormtrooper Finn.

00:00:56.519 --> 00:01:00.199
Plus, there were the welcome returns of Han
Solo and now-General Leia.

00:01:00.199 --> 00:01:04.500
Harrison Ford's older-and-wiser Han was a
highlight, and the revelation that Kylo Ren

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:09.340
had been born Ben Solo to Han and Leia raised
the film's dramatic stakes considerably.

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:13.520
The scene in which Han goes to confront his
son on a bridge spanning a seemingly bottomless

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:18.020
abyss is fraught with tension, and for a moment,
Haan appears to be getting through to his

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:20.060
son, until Ren does the unthinkable.

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:26.640
It's been speculated by fans that Han may
have gone to his death willingly, in order

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:31.110
to help his son transition fully to the dark
side so he can successfully infiltrate and

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:32.560
bring down the First Order.

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:36.310
But even if this turns out to be the case,
it doesn't make this scene any less shocking,

00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:37.740
abrupt, or upsetting.

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:40.640
And it wasn't even the first heart-wrenching
death in Star Wars.

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:42.420
Star Wars: A New Hope

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:48.140
When Star Wars was released to theaters in
1977, nobody had ever seen anything even remotely

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:49.340
like it.

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:52.720
We all know about the cultural juggernaut
it became, but it's impossible to overstate

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:56.280
what a blast of filmmaking ingenuity it represented
at the time.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:01.770
The project was almost shelved pre-release
due to its budget and perceived lack of marketability.

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:06.670
Also surprising was just how many young moviegoers
were traumatized during the film's most heart-rending

00:02:06.670 --> 00:02:09.660
moment — Darth Vader's vanquishing of Obi-Wan
Kenobi.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:10.940
"You can't win Darth.

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:16.860
If you strike me down I'll become more powerful
than you could possibly imagine"

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:21.620
Audiences were left with their jaws on the
floor when the Jedi Master lowered his defenses

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:23.500
as Luke Skywalker looked on.

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:27.380
Luke's reaction mirrored the audience's, and
even the Rebels' eventual triumph during the

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:31.610
film's conclusion wasn't entirely enough to
wipe away the sting of Kenobi's seemingly

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:32.720
senseless death.

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:35.260
Indiana Jones and the Temple of Doom

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:40.230
Raiders of the Lost Ark became an instant
classic upon its 1981 release, and there was

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:44.670
never any doubt that audiences would get much,
much more of the adventures of Indiana Jones.

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:49.220
The climax of that film was pretty traumatizing
in its own right, what with the angry spirits

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:50.460
and Nazi face-melting.

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:55.720
But 1984's Indiana Jones and the Temple of
Doom promised to go quite a bit darker.

00:02:55.720 --> 00:03:00.890
It was the film that created the PG-13 rating,
largely due to one unexpected, jaw-droppingly

00:03:00.890 --> 00:03:01.959
violent scene.

00:03:01.959 --> 00:03:06.370
It comes fairly early in the film, as Jones
and sidekicks Willie Scott and Short Round

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:11.100
observe a sacrificial ritual being performed
by the villainous Mola Ram and his Thuggee

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:11.600
cult.

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:13.960
"ah"

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:23.120
The scene traumatized young fans and received
strong criticism.

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:27.620
The New York Times called the film "too violent"
based partly on that scene - and probably

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:29.040
that one where they ate the monkey brains.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:31.520
E.T. the Extra-Terrestrial

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:35.950
Steven Spielberg has never been unwilling
to toy with the feelings of his audience.

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:39.540
Even when giving his characters the happiest
of endings, he's skilled at drawing genuine

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:41.160
performances out of his actors.

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:46.710
His 1982 masterpiece E.T. the Extra-Terrestrial
is a bit of an emotional rollercoaster.

00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:50.680
Its climactic scene, in which young Elliott
and his friends evade federal agents in order

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:55.540
to help E.T. get home, manages to be suspenseful,
action-filled, and hilarious at once.

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:59.450
But it's not until the threat is over and
E.T. is about to return safely home that the

00:03:59.450 --> 00:04:00.460
waterworks start.

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:05.960
"I'll be right here"

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:11.240
It's not so much the delivery of the animatronic
alien as it is the absolutely heart-crushing

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:16.130
reaction shots of Henry Thomas, Drew Barrymore,
and and Dee Wallace, literally falling to

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:17.620
her knees with emotion.

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:21.249
Even bullying big brother Michael appears
to be holding back the tears, and for many

00:04:21.249 --> 00:04:25.560
young viewers, the scene would have represented
their first exposure to genuine heartbreak

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:26.060
and loss.

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:27.020
It

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:32.700
Stephen King's novel It is a catalog of pure,
unadulterated terror, and widely considered

00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:34.289
one of his finest works.

00:04:34.289 --> 00:04:38.219
The book had already received an acceptable
live-action adaptation in the form of the

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:44.240
1990 TV miniseries, so the 2017 adaptation
was viewed with a healthy amount of skepticism.

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:48.240
Fortunately, the cast and crew delivered not
only a ferocious new vision, but one of the

00:04:48.250 --> 00:04:53.020
very best horror films of the last decade,
thanks in no small part to Bill Skarsgard's

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:55.460
absolutely masterful portrayal of Pennywise.

00:04:55.460 --> 00:04:59.660
Even viewers already familiar with the novel
or miniseries couldn't help being shocked

00:04:59.669 --> 00:05:01.990
to their cores by the character's introduction.

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:06.400
Young Georgie loses his paper boat in a storm
drain, only to see the clown's predatory eyes

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:08.380
peering at him from out of the drain's darkness.

00:05:08.380 --> 00:05:12.419
Over the course of their conversation, the
clown convinces the boy to reach on in and

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:13.220
grab his boat.

00:05:13.240 --> 00:05:17.620
It's the last decision Georgie will ever make,
and the graphic nature of his demise makes

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:21.120
the scene land with a punch that no television
adaptation could ever match.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:27.580
Avengers: Infinity War

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:32.000
Marvel fans were thrilled when the studio
reached a deal allowing for joint custody

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:35.279
of Marvel's flagship character: Peter Parker,
the amazing Spider-Man.

00:05:35.279 --> 00:05:40.479
They were even more excited when the MCU incarnation
of Spidey lived up to any expectation they

00:05:40.479 --> 00:05:41.650
possibly could have had.

00:05:41.650 --> 00:05:45.800
Tom Holland's endlessly wisecracking version
of the wall-crawler hit the sweet spot with

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:50.440
fans that had been left somewhat cold by Sam
Raimi's Spider-Man trilogy and Marc Webb's

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:51.280
reboots.

00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:54.960
News of Holland's six-picture contract meant
that we would be treated to Spidey's adventures

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:56.779
in the MCU for years to come.

00:05:56.779 --> 00:06:01.051
Then we were given Avengers: Infinity War,
the lead-up to the end of the MCU's first

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:02.920
three-phase Thanos mega-arc.

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:04.610
We all knew there would be blood.

00:06:04.610 --> 00:06:08.599
Thanos' killing of Loki and Heimdall in the
picture's first five minutes confirmed that

00:06:08.599 --> 00:06:12.240
the Mad Titan was a threat unlike any the
Avengers had yet faced.

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:16.520
But few expected the carnage of the film's
climax, in which Thanos actually manages to

00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:21.119
succeed in wiping out half of all life in
the universe with one snap of his fingers.

00:06:21.119 --> 00:06:25.270
Watching fan favorites like Doctor Strange
and Black Panther crumble to dust was bad

00:06:25.270 --> 00:06:26.270
enough.

00:06:26.270 --> 00:06:27.820
But Spidey's demise was utterly crushing.

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:29.220
"I'm sorry."

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:38.460
Of course, he'll be back but in that moment,
it didn't matter.

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:39.980
Serenity

00:06:40.360 --> 00:06:46.219
The Fox TV series Firefly aired for only one
season in 2002, but all these years later,

00:06:46.219 --> 00:06:48.810
its imprint on pop culture refuses to fade.

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:53.490
The story of an intergalactic band of ne'er-do-wells
on the run from the galactic Alliance introduced

00:06:53.490 --> 00:06:56.820
us to the crew of the Firefly-class spaceship
Serenity.

00:06:56.820 --> 00:07:01.319
This included sardonic Captain Malcolm "Mal"
Reynolds, his second-in-command Zoe Washburne,

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:05.260
and her husband Hoban "Wash" Washburne, the
ship's wisecracking pilot.

00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:10.440
After the series' abrupt cancellation, creator
Joss Whedon gave fans a proper sendoff with

00:07:10.449 --> 00:07:13.399
the 2005 theatrical release Serenity.

00:07:13.399 --> 00:07:17.219
But he also saw fit to give them a healthy
dose of heartbreak while he was at it.

00:07:17.219 --> 00:07:21.729
Late in the film, the crew succeeds in provoking
the Alliance into a battle with the animalistic

00:07:21.729 --> 00:07:25.949
Reavers, and Wash manages to pilot the damaged
Serenity through the melee, landing on the

00:07:25.949 --> 00:07:29.089
surface of the planet over which the battle
is taking place.

00:07:29.089 --> 00:07:33.080
Just at the moment all appears safe, Wash
utters his final words before being impaled

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:37.340
by a Reaver spear, dying instantly in front
of his horrified wife and Captain.

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:39.480
"I'm a leaf on the wind"

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:46.640
"Wash!"

00:07:47.460 --> 00:07:51.699
Whedon is well-known for bestowing cruel fates
upon beloved characters, but the death of

00:07:51.699 --> 00:07:55.530
Wash is one that even his most ardent fans
have a hard time forgiving him for.

